      "my_answer": "Otolaryngology (ENT) for definitive surgical drainage, with emergency medicine for initial stabilization.",
      "rationale": "Peritonsillar abscess typically requires incision and drainage, which is best performed by an ENT specialist. Emergency physicians may initiate management but should consult ENT promptly."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the key differential diagnoses to consider?",
      "my_answer": "Peritonsillar cellulitis, retropharyngeal abscess, parapharyngeal abscess, infectious mononucleosis with severe tonsillar hypertrophy, and epiglottitis.",
      "rationale": "These conditions can present with similar symptoms of severe sore throat, dysphagia, and trismus, but require different management (e.g., antibiotics alone for cellulitis, airway protection for epiglottitis)."
    },
