  "sub_questions": [
    {
      "id": "Q1",
      "question": "What is the most likely diagnosis based on the presentation of localized eyelid tenderness and erythema near the lid margin without other ocular findings?",
      "my_answer": "External hordeolum (stye) – an acute infection of an eyelid gland (likely Zeis or Moll) at the lid margin.",
      "rationale": "The acute onset, localized pain, erythema, and tenderness at the lid margin in the absence of diffuse swelling, vision changes, or trauma are classic for a stye."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "question": "What is the urgency of this condition?",
      "my_answer": "Self-care appropriate; non-urgent.",
      "rationale": "A simple stye is typically self-limited and can be managed with home measures. There are no signs of preseptal or orbital cellulitis, which would require urgent care."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "question": "Which specialty should this patient be routed to if needed?",
      "my_answer": "Ophthalmology (or primary care with ophthalmology referral if not improving).",
      "rationale": "Eyelid lesions are within the scope of ophthalmology, though many can be managed by general practitioners. Specialty referral is appropriate if complications or lack of resolution occur."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the key differential diagnoses to consider?",
      "my_answer": "Chalazion (non-infectious lipogranuloma), internal hordeolum, preseptal cellulitis, early orbital cellulitis, or rarely sebaceous cell carcinoma.",
      "rationale": "These conditions can present with eyelid swelling/erythema. The localized nature and lack of systemic symptoms favor stye over cellulitis, while chalazion is usually less painful and more chronic."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5",
      "question": "What red flags would indicate a more serious condition requiring urgent evaluation?",
      "my_answer": "Progressive diffuse eyelid swelling, fever, pain with eye movement, decreased vision, proptosis, or ophthalmoplegia.",
      "rationale": "These suggest preseptal or orbital cellulitis, which are emergencies. Their absence in this case supports a benign stye."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q6",
      "question": "What diagnostics are indicated in this case?",
      "my_answer": "None; diagnosis is clinical. No imaging or laboratory tests are needed.",
      "rationale": "The presentation is classic for a stye. Unnecessary tests are avoided unless red flags or atypical features emerge."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q7",
      "question": "What is the initial management for this patient?",
      "my_answer": "Warm compresses 3-4 times daily for 10-15 minutes, gentle lid hygiene, and avoidance of squeezing. Over-the-counter analgesics if needed.",
      "rationale": "Warm compresses promote drainage and relief. Topical antibiotics are not routinely required for simple external hordeola. Incision and drainage is reserved for persistent cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q8",
      "question": "When should the patient be reassessed or referred?",
      "my_answer": "If no improvement in 48 hours, or if symptoms worsen, or if red flags develop, refer to ophthalmology.",
      "rationale": "Most styes resolve within a few days. Persistent or worsening symptoms warrant specialist evaluation to rule out complications or alternative diagnoses."
    }
  ],
